How many widget-test bodies check nothing but
`expect(tester.takeException(), isNull)`, less the ones excused with a
reason in `scripts/check_thin_assertions.py`.

A CEILING, and it may only fall. IT HAS REACHED ZERO, which is the only
kind nobody has to remember to lower -- the same shape
`check_counted_assertions.py` arrived at, and for the same reason.

It started at 34, all of them in `dialogs_build_batch2_test.dart`, and
every one has been rewritten. On the way: four wrong-shape fixtures (the
modifier groups drawing the four characters n-u-l-l, "What null bills", a
filing fixture that was a row from another table, and a forecast sheet
handed three of twenty-three columns) and two real defects (an approval
rule naming an invited member who had never accepted, and a test that
opened no dialog at all because the throwing fake made `resolveSupplier`
return before it drew).

It came down 34 -> 14 -> 11 -> 7 -> 6 -> 5 -> 0, and EVERY step after the
first was the gate refusing the stale number rather than somebody
remembering to lower it. That under-ceiling arm is the half of a ratchet
nobody writes and the half that keeps the ground won.

## What a zero ceiling means for the next test

A new body that checks only `takeException` now fails CI. The remedy is
to assert what the screen SAYS -- or, where "it did not throw" genuinely
is the whole claim, to name the test in `ALLOWED` with a reason. Thirteen
are named there: twelve overflow tests, where the framework raising on
`RenderFlex` IS the assertion and `takeException` is what collects it,
and one that writes a spreadsheet `check_xlsx.py` reads back in the same
job.
